format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-and-the-body

lesson time-distance-shielding
title: Time, distance, shielding
minutes: 6
xp: 50
teaches: u4-three-ways
teaches: u4-pick-the-way
relies_on: u4-small-spread
relies_on: u3-gamma-shielding

description:
    There are three ways to lower a dose. You will meet each one, then spot which one a real situation is using.

cite ref-epa-protecting-yourself

supports:
    Limiting time, increasing distance and shielding all reduce a dose; barriers of lead, concrete or water protect from gamma rays; dentists place a lead apron on patients; more walls give more protection.

card c01
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: You can lower a dose
figure: three-ways
figure_at: 1

body:
    A dose is not fixed. There are three ways to lower it: spend less time near the source, stay farther from it, or put a shield between you and it. Time, distance and shielding.

card c02
kind: item
type: item
item: u4-l4-tom-nothing
connected: true

card c03
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Less time
figure: less-time
figure_at: 1

body:
    Limiting the time you are exposed reduces the dose from the source. A shorter visit near a source means a smaller dose.

card c04
kind: item
type: item
item: u4-l4-choice-time
connected: true

card c05
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: More distance
figure: more-distance
figure_at: 1

body:
    The dose decreases dramatically as you increase your distance from the source. Stepping farther away lowers it a lot.

card c06
kind: item
type: item
item: u4-l4-choice-distance
connected: true

card c07
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Something in between
figure: something-between
figure_at: 1

body:
    Shielding means putting material between you and the source. Barriers of lead, concrete or water protect from penetrating gamma rays, and a good shield can greatly reduce or remove the dose.

card c08
kind: item
type: item
item: u4-l4-fill-three

card c09
kind: teach
type: explain
scored: 0
title: Where you see them
figure: real-uses
figure_at: 1

body:
    Dentists place a lead apron on patients having X-rays of their teeth. Radioactive materials are stored under water, in concrete, or in lead-lined rooms. In a shelter, more walls give more protection.

